ing to be found  heather urex west global news calgary  i had the new program designed to give  black canadian families a leg up to home  ownership  you're watching global national  a new program to help more black  families break into the housing market  has been launched in toronto the average  home ownership rate for black households  in canada is about 40 compared to the  overall rate of nearly 70 percent the  shared equity initiative is the first of  its kind in canada and as ann gaviola  reports if it's successful it'll expand  to other canadian cities  oh it would mean legacy  it would mean financial security it  would mean stability and it would mean  something to pass on to my children  rather than my bills regina magambo  dreams of homeownership the single  mother of three young children as a  small business owner her hair extension  and beauty product retail business was  hit by coveted restrictions reducing her  annual household income to 65 thousand  dollars she pre-qualified for a 600  thousand dollar mortgage but says that  isn't enough to afford a home for her  family in this ultra competitive market  you just never know what the cost of a  home is until after a bidding war  happens  the average selling price for a home in  the gta is now 1.3 million the black  north homeownership bridge program  brings together developers willing to  offer dedicated homes at a discount and  funding from charities and government  including 10 million from ottawa the  shared equity program provides support  towards a down payment and lower monthly  mortgage payments for 200 families with  young children to own a home in the gta  black north is currently accepting  applicants and in discussion with donors  our objective is to raise about 65  million dollars  and that will help us because we want to  create not only in the gta and greater  toronto area we want to take this  program right across canada the program  involves twin mortgages one from a  traditional lender a bank or a credit  union to be paid off in full first the  second loan is with black north to  bridge the affordability gap we are  building a different playbook at how to  address anti-black racism and we wanted  to build a program that was going to  create wealth and that was going to  create mobility for the black community  magambo says homeownership would be  transformational for her family for  generations to come it would create  opportunities for my children in the  future so that means the world to me it  lets me know that i wouldn't be working  in vain but that i would be working  towards something ann gaviola global  news toronto  up next pinching pierogies to help the  victims of war  in communities across the country  ukrainian canadians are coming together  to pinch pierogies or petohe as my  ukrainian mother taught me they're  making the food to raise money for  victims of war and as carolyn curry de  castillo tells us the humble pierogi has  a history of helping  [Music]  they sing a traditional ukrainian song  of resistance while preparing a food  that's nourished so many generations in  ukraine potatoes and usually with fried  onions  my favorites are though with the  sauerkraut those progress yeah  favorite oh yeah all time favorite but  we make them only for  usually christmas time volunteers at  assumption of the blessed virgin mary  church in calgary assembling thousands  of pierogies and selling them to raise  money for relief efforts making the  parochial is very soothing there's  something soothing about it having the  dough in your hands and making it  christine musienko is the daughter of  ukrainian immigrants every day she talks  to relatives who are now in bomb  shelters it's my  last phone call when i go to sleep it's  my first phone call when i wake up there  is a long tradition of ukrainian  canadians shaping pierogies to raise  money last year we had a fundraiser for  our church and we made over sixty  thousand dollars selling pierogies this  church was built from  women making pierogies we used to make  them every sunday oksana david not only  about her relatives who were enduring  this war but those who suffered the  horror of the 1930s in her homeland she  worries about possible shortages now in  the bread basket of europe and how  quickly a situation of food abundance  can change anywhere and i can't believe  that people in ukraine in a land that's  so rich and can feed in a whole europe  people could be starving like they were  starving  90 years ago when stalin was trying to  basically extinguish ukrainian people  [Music]  making a food that goes back centuries  allowing those in calgary helplessly  ringing their hands to put them to work  helping from afar carolyn curry de  castillo global news  [Applause]  and that is global national for this  sunday night i'm colleen christie  tonight we leave you with support and  solidarity for ukraine in sports and on  the streets  [Applause]  from montreal to toronto people across  canada rallied outside russian  consulates calling for peace  a lot of nods to the crisis in ukraine  and in vancouver major league soccer  fans watching the white caps waved  ukrainian flags while players wore  t-shirts with the words we stand with  ukraine during the warm-up thanks for  watching donna friesen will be back  tomorrow  [Music]  [Music]  the
 

